{"name":"Les Demoiselles d’Avignon","hex":"#e59d7b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"les","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Lese."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Pronunciation spelling of let's"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/les"},{"term":"demoiselles","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of demoiselle"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/demoiselles"}],"entries":[{"title":"Les Demoiselles d'Avignon","blocks":[{"text":"Les Demoiselles d'Avignon is a large oil painting created in 1907 by the Spanish artist Pablo Picasso. Part of the permanent collection of the Museum of Modern Art in New York, it portrays five nude female prostitutes in a brothel on Carrer d'Avinyó, a street in Barcelona, Spain. The figures are confrontational and not conventionally feminine, being rendered with angular and disjointed body shapes, some to a menacing degree. The far left figure exhibits facial features and dress of Egyptian or southern Asian style. The two adjacent figures are in an Iberian style of Picasso's Spain, while the two on the right have African mask-like features. Picasso said the ethnic primitivism evoked in these masks moved him to \"liberate an utterly original artistic style of compelling, even savage force\" leading him to add a shamanistic aspect to his project."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4c/Les_Demoiselles_d%27Avignon.jpg/330px-Les_Demoiselles_d%27Avignon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Demoiselles_d'Avignon","wikidata":"Q910199"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Le_Grand_Baigneur%2C_par_Paul_C%C3%A9zanne%2C_Yorck.jpg/500px-Le_Grand_Baigneur%2C_par_Paul_C%C3%A9zanne%2C_Yorck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Le_Grand_Baigneur%2C_par_Paul_C%C3%A9zanne%2C_Yorck.jpg/1280px-Le_Grand_Baigneur%2C_par_Paul_C%C3%A9zanne%2C_Yorck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Le_Grand_Baigneur%2C_par_Paul_C%C3%A9zanne%2C_Yorck.jpg/500px-Le_Grand_Baigneur%2C_par_Paul_C%C3%A9zanne%2C_Yorck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Paul Cézanne, Bather, 1885–1887, Museum of Modern Art, formerly collection Lillie P. Bliss","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALe_Grand_Baigneur%2C_par_Paul_C%C3%A9zanne%2C_Yorck.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Saint_Martin_and_the_Beggar_%28c1597-1600%29_by_El_Greco_-_Chicago.jpg/330px-Saint_Martin_and_the_Beggar_%28c1597-1600%29_by_El_Greco_-_Chicago.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Saint_Martin_and_the_Beggar_%28c1597-1600%29_by_El_Greco_-_Chicago.jpg/330px-Saint_Martin_and_the_Beggar_%28c1597-1600%29_by_El_Greco_-_Chicago.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaint_Martin_and_the_Beggar_(c1597-1600)_by_El_Greco_-_Chicago.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/03/Boy_Leading_a_Horse.jpg/500px-Boy_Leading_a_Horse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/03/Boy_Leading_a_Horse.jpg/500px-Boy_Leading_a_Horse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoy_Leading_a_Horse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bonheur_Matisse.jpg/500px-Bonheur_Matisse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bonheur_Matisse.jpg/500px-Bonheur_Matisse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Henri Matisse, Le bonheur de vivre (1905–06), oil on canvas, 175 × 241 cm. Barnes Foundation, Merion, PA. A painting that was called Fauvist and brought Matisse both public derision and notoriety. Hilton Kramer wrote: \"owing to its long sequestration in the collection of the Barnes Foundation, which never permitted its reproduction in color, it is the least familiar of modern masterpieces. Yet this painting was Matisse's own response to the hostility his work had met with in the Salon d'Automne of 1905.\"[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABonheur_Matisse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Rousseau-Hungry-Lion.jpg/500px-Rousseau-Hungry-Lion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Rousseau-Hungry-Lion.jpg/1280px-Rousseau-Hungry-Lion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Rousseau-Hungry-Lion.jpg/500px-Rousseau-Hungry-Lion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Henri Rousseau, The Hungry Lion Throws Itself on the Antelope, 1905, was the reason for the term Fauvism, and the original \"Wild Beast\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARousseau-Hungry-Lion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Matisse_Souvenir_de_Biskra.jpg/500px-Matisse_Souvenir_de_Biskra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Matisse_Souvenir_de_Biskra.jpg/1280px-Matisse_Souvenir_de_Biskra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb